    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c14d6c7a_3be42760",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 122,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-03T23:22:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I guess it\u0027s more accurate to say the bits automatically clear on read, if the underlying condition also cleared.\nIt\u0027s probably best to document the observed behavior of ALERT_L (it\u0027s level sensitive, asserted as long as any condition that triggers alert is still active, etc).",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 122,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 122,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "5a8a22836a5d9056ad4c265217f395aa90b8aea0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "51b82732_40a267ee",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 185,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-03T21:33:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The status and control4 values are stale at this point, so you\u0027re always going to execute this path one extra time.\n\nThe SYV documentation isn\u0027t very explicit on the behavior of the ALERT_L pin.  It states \"The output remains asserted until all registers of the device are read by I2C control.\" But it doesn\u0027t state what happens to ALERT_L if the condition that triggered the interrupt is still active.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 181,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 185,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "5a8a22836a5d9056ad4c265217f395aa90b8aea0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d17add6a_da3b117f",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 185,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-03T22:04:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes I played with this quite a bit and didn\u0027t know the best way to go about it. Since the EC pin interrupts are edge-triggered as far as I can tell, it will not trigger another interrupt if the alerts weren\u0027t cleared. An equivalent check here would be checking the alert_l pin status, but that only works if it is not a shared interrupt. If its shared we can\u0027t know if the interrupts were actually cleared. So I stuck with this.\n\nIf you look at the sn5s330 driver that is how it works (check alert pin). I think it is a bit different though since it has registers for the rise and fall of alert bits. \n\nTo answer your question, from experimentation, the ALERT_L will not de-assert if the alert condition is still present, even if the register is read. It seems that the ALERT_L pin directly tracks the alert registers, and they will not clear unless the condition is gone.\n\nOne thing I would like to do is add a delay to this, unfortunately that means we could miss FRS events.",
      "parentUuid": "51b82732_40a267ee",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 181,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 185,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "5a8a22836a5d9056ad4c265217f395aa90b8aea0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ee5c8778_28e89e5e",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 185,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-03T22:50:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, EC interrupts are always edge triggered (I believe some ECs don\u0027t even support level-triggering).  That\u0027s why we ended up with the sn5s330 driver the way it is.\n\nIf the line doesn\u0027t de-assert until the condition is cleared, does that leave you in the position of possibly missing something important because the interrupt triggered on something that\u0027s filtered out, and then something important came in later without the line de-asserting?",
      "parentUuid": "d17add6a_da3b117f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 181,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 185,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "5a8a22836a5d9056ad4c265217f395aa90b8aea0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fff3a7a1_58cae4c6",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 185,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-03T23:22:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "At a minimum, you need to re-read the STATUS and CONTROL_4 regs again before doing this check.  Since I2C accesses can be slow, it\u0027s probably best to check ALERT_L directly when the board defines  CONFIG_USBC_PPC_DEDICATED_INT.  Otherwise poll the STATUS and CONTROL_4 regs.\n\nSomething like.\nif (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_USBC_PPC_DEDICATED_INT) \u0026\u0026\n    ppc_get_alert_status(port)) {\n  syv682x_interrupt(port);\n} else {\n  read_reg(port, SYV682X_CONTROL_4_REG, \u0026control4);\n  read_reg(port, SYV682X_STATUS_REG, \u0026status);\n  if (status \u0026 SYV682X_STATUS_INT_MASK ||\n      control4 \u0026 SYV682X_CONTROL_4_INT_MASK) {\n    syv682x_interrupt(port);\n  }\n}\n\nUltimately, we probably want to rate limit this.  The corner case of FRS occurring while a fault is active, probably isn\u0027t worth it.",
      "parentUuid": "ee5c8778_28e89e5e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 181,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 185,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "5a8a22836a5d9056ad4c265217f395aa90b8aea0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "94682049_e509ba22",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 185,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-04T00:16:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Okay thanks, I think you\u0027re right that its not worth it. \n\nIs the preferred way to delay to add a delay in the hook_call_deferred, or by doing msleep here? I\u0027m guessing msleep would be blocking.\n\nTo answer Diana\u0027s question, yes we could miss something. We have no way to know when a new interrupt comes in unless we poll. The good news is all the alert conditions are bad, and we should never really be operating in that state. The exception is FRS, but that is guaranteed to clear on read.\n\nThe corner case I was worried about was the error condition going away, then getting an FRS (which is the only interrupt we really need to deal with).  We don\u0027t know the error condition went away without reading the register, and it won\u0027t generate an interrupt since it was never cleared.",
      "parentUuid": "fff3a7a1_58cae4c6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 181,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 185,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "5a8a22836a5d9056ad4c265217f395aa90b8aea0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
